 "title": "Who Shares What From Whom: An Ego-Network Conjoint of Misinformation Sharing",
 "authors": [
  "Yamil R. Velez"
 ],
 "summary": "Does the type of information in a headline change whether people choose to share it and whether they judge it more plausible? We analyse a US conjoint experiment on a YouGov sample (12,000 responses) in which respondents made binary forced choices between headlines. Headlines were Misinformation, Fact-check, or True/neutral (placebo, the reference). Relative to True/neutral, Misinformation was chosen for sharing 18.2 percentage points less often (95% CI [−20.4, −16.0]) and Fact-check 13.3 points less often (95% CI [−15.6, −11.1]). Plausibility choices followed the same pattern: Misinformation was 18.6 points lower (95% CI [−20.8, −16.4]) and Fact-check 12.4 points lower (95% CI [−14.7, −10.2]). All four tests were two-sided with p<0.001. Both hypotheses carry an undocumented deviation from",
